Polyarteritis nodosa
Rheumatology & Immunology
Mononeuritis multiplex, Abdominal angina, Mesenteric microaneurysms

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-032
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, agonizing abdominal pain shortly after eating, combined with a new right-sided foot drop and profound muscle aches, requiring immediate evaluation to rule out acute mesenteric ischemia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with sudden-onset neurological weakness and agonizing post-prandial abdominal pain. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to assess for an acute surgical abdomen resulting from vasculitic bowel ischemia, confirm mononeuritis multiplex, and arrange immediate emergency transfer. Delaying until standard daytime hours posed an imminent, fatal risk of transmural bowel infarction and retroperitoneal hemorrhage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Thirty minutes after I ate dinner my stomach started cramping so badly I thought I would pass out, and now I can't lift my right foot to walk."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-week history of profound constitutional symptoms including an unexplained low-grade fever, severe myalgias (particularly in the calves), and a 4kg unintentional weight loss. This evening, the patient experienced the sudden onset of a right foot drop (mononeuritis multiplex). Shortly after a meal, the patient developed severe, escalating abdominal angina, described as a generalized, deep, agonizing ache. Furthermore, the patient reports unexplained new-onset headaches over the last week. The spouse notes a prior history of chronic Hepatitis B infection. The patient completely denies any cough, shortness of breath, or sinus issues (sparing of the pulmonary vasculature).
Functional Status: Severely distressed, lying perfectly still in bed; unable to walk due to foot drop and abdominal agony.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of catastrophic bowel necrosis or microaneurysm rupture; spouse is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/100 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely toxic, writhing intermittently with waves of abdominal pain, pale, and diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Diffusely tender to deep palpation. Bowel sounds are diminished. No rigid guarding yet, but marked voluntary guarding is present due to severe visceral pain. The discrepancy between the severity of the pain and the relatively soft abdomen strongly suggests mesenteric ischemia. Neurological: Profound weakness (1/5) in right ankle dorsiflexion and eversion (right peroneal nerve palsy). Reflexes are preserved proximally. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic. Elevated blood pressure (175/100) noted, suspicious for new-onset hypertension due to renal artery involvement (renovascular hypertension). Dermatological: Prominent livedo reticularis on the lower extremities; several tender, 1cm subcutaneous nodules palpated on the calves. The diagnostic tetrad is highly evident clinically: mononeuritis multiplex, severe abdominal angina, predicting the discovery of mesenteric microaneurysms on hospital angiography and transmural necrotizing inflammation on biopsy.
Environmental Observations: Patient requires immediate extraction; physical environment is safe for paramedic access.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; paracetamol provided zero relief for the abdominal pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polyarteritis nodosa - Status: Exacerbated (Acute mesenteric ischemia and vasculitic neuropathy)
Differentials:

Thromboembolic Mesenteric Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a classic multi-system vasculitic presentation, specifically the tender subcutaneous nodules and sudden mononeuritis multiplex (foot drop), which characterize PAN rather than a pure embolic event from atrial fibrillation.

Microscopic Polyangiitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete sparing of the pulmonary system (no hemoptysis or crackles) and the presence of severe abdominal angina (classic for medium-vessel PAN) rather than the pulmonary-renal focus of MPA.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is diffuse and post-prandial rather than localized to the right lower quadrant, combined with the defining presence of new peripheral nerve palsy and profound new-onset hypertension.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Imminent bowel necrosis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering a fulminant multi-system vasculitic crisis, characterized by acute mesenteric ischemia (abdominal angina), mononeuritis multiplex, and hypertensive crisis. The combination of post-prandial abdominal agony and foot drop in a Hepatitis B-positive patient is classic for Polyarteritis Nodosa affecting medium-sized vessels. An urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely mandated to physically triage the acute abdomen, document the neurological deficit, and facilitate an immediate 000 emergency extraction for surgical and rheumatological intervention. Waiting for standard hours would result in fatal bowel infarction or massive retroperitoneal hemorrhage, definitively fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is in an acute ischemic crisis and requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 ambulance. The hospital must urgently perform a CT angiogram of the abdomen to assess for mesenteric microaneurysms, infarcts, or hemorrhage, alongside renal function testing and Hepatitis B serology. Immediate high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids and potentially cyclophosphamide are required to halt the necrotizing vasculitis, with surgical consultation for the threatened bowel.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict Nil By Mouth (NBM) enforcement: Immediately cease all oral intake, including water; explicitly linking this absolute restriction to the fact that digesting food is causing the agonizing stomach pain by demanding blood flow that the damaged arteries cannot supply. Positional bracing: Remain lying flat with knees slightly bent; demonstrating that this specific posture relieves tension on the abdominal wall, providing slight relief from the ischemic pain while awaiting the ambulance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; acute surgical/critical care admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission expected.

